1.m:
"One sits causing the abundance of the verses to thrive; one sings the Gāyatra song to the Śakvarī meters; / the Brahmā, one of them, declares the knowledge of origin; and one measures out the measure of the sacrifice."
Thus it describes the distribution of the priests' functions. "One sits causing the abundance of the verses to thrive": the Hotṛ priest. A verse [ṛc] is to be praised [arcanī]. "One sings the Gāyatra song to the Śakvarī meters": the Udgātṛ priest. "Gāyatra" is from [the root] gai- (to sing) in the sense of praising [stutikarman]. The "Śakvarī" verses are from [the root] śak- (to be able); for it is known [in the Brāhmaṇa]: "Because he was able [aśakat] to slay Vṛtra with them, that is the Śakvarī-nature of the Śakvarī verses." "The Brahmā, one of them, declares knowledge at every birth [jāte jāte]": the Brahmā knows all; he is worthy to know everything; the Brahmā is fortified [parivṛḍha] by learning; Brahman is fortified everywhere. "One measures out the measure of the sacrifice": the Adhvaryu priest. "Adhvaryu" is one who desires the sacrifice [adhvarayu]; he enjoins the sacrifice [adhvaraṃ yunakti]; he is the leader of the sacrifice; or he longs for the sacrifice. Or else, when it denotes a student, "yu" is an adjunct.
"Adhvara" is a name for sacrifice; "dhvarati" is a verb of harming; [adhvara] is its negation. Some say [tva] is a particle. But how could that which inherently lacks an accent be a noun? Yet it is observed to undergo inflection:
"And they call one firmly imbibed in friendship"—in the accusative case;
"And to one she opened her body"—in the dative case;
and also in the nominative plural:

3.m:
"Through you, who make us prosper well, O Brahmaṇaspati, may we mortals obtain desirable treasures;
Whichever foes are far from us, and whichever are near, crush those destitute ones!"
"Through you, the promoter of our welfare, O Brahmaṇaspati, may we receive desirable treasures for mortals; and whichever foes of ours are far, and whichever *taḷitaḥ* are near—*arātayaḥ* means either those whose nature is non-giving (*adāna-karmāṇaḥ*), or those whose mind is devoid of generosity (*adāna-prajñāḥ*)—crush those destitute ones (*anapnasaḥ*)." *Apnaḥ* is a name for form (or property), from the fact that one attains it (*āpnoti*). Śākapūṇi says that lightning is *taḷit*, for it strikes down (*avatāḷayati*), and it is seen from afar; however, here the word for near is definitely intended: "Though being far away, you shine brightly as if near (*taḷid iva*)"—even being far away, you appear as if near. The subsequent eighteen words are names for the thunderbolt (*vajraḥ*). Why *vajraḥ*? From the fact that it excludes/leaves out (*varjayatīti sataḥ*). There, *kutsaḥ* is from *kṛntati* (to cut). Kutsa is a seer, the composer (*kartā*) of hymns of praise—so says Aupamanyava; moreover, it is also simply a verb of killing: "as his friend, Indra slew Śuṣṇa." The subsequent four roots are verbs of exercising lordship. The subsequent four words are names for lord (*īśvaraḥ*). There, *inaḥ* is either one who has attained (*sanitaḥ*) through lordship, or lordship has been attained (*sanitam*) by him.
